---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Passwordless Onboarding">
  <h1>Passwordless Onboarding</h1>
  <p>
    Skip the password step entirely: invite a user via email, let them
    redeem the invitation in the browser, and immediately enroll a passkey.
    From that point on, login is biometrics-only.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 1 — Admin invites the user</h2>
  <p>
    The admin uses <code>InviteUser</code> with
    <code>create_immediately: false</code>. A throwaway random password is
    used internally so we have a hash on file (the proto requires it),
    but the user never sees it.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST https://auth.acme.com/identity.IdentityService/InviteUser \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"new@acme.com","name":"New Hire","role":"member"}' | jq .`} lang="bash" />
  <Code code={`{
  "user":            { "status": "USER_STATUS_INVITED", ... },
  "invitationToken": "inv_...",
  "setupUrl":        "https://app.acme.com/accept?token=inv_..."
}`} lang="json" />
  <p>The admin emails the <code>setupUrl</code> to the user.</p>

  <h2>Step 2 — User redeems the invitation</h2>
  <p>
    The user clicks the link. The front-end calls
    <code>AcceptInvitation</code>, which sets a password and returns
    access + refresh tokens.
  </p>
  <Code code={`// In the /accept route:
const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
const token = params.get("token");
const password = crypto.randomUUID() + crypto.randomUUID(); // throwaway

const accept = await client.acceptInvitation({
  invitationToken: token,
  password,
  name: "",  // keep the admin-supplied name
});

storeTokens(accept.accessToken, accept.refreshToken);`} lang="typescript" />

  <h2>Step 3 — Enroll a passkey immediately</h2>
  <Code code={`const begin = await client.beginPasskeyRegistration({ deviceName: "First device" });

const credential = await navigator.credentials.create(JSON.parse(begin.optionsJson));

await client.completePasskeyRegistration({
  challengeId: begin.challengeId,
  credentialJson: JSON.stringify(credential),
  deviceName: "First device",
});`} lang="typescript" />

  <h2>Step 4 — Forget the password</h2>
  <p>
    The throwaway password is never shown to the user. To prevent any
    future password-login path, set
    <code>GATEWAY_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL=false</code> on the deployment, or
    keep <code>true</code> and rely on the user never knowing the
    password.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 5 — Subsequent logins</h2>
  <Code code={`const begin = await client.beginPasskeyLogin({ email: "" });
const credential = await navigator.credentials.get(JSON.parse(begin.optionsJson));
const result = await client.completePasskeyLogin({
  challengeId: begin.challengeId,
  credentialJson: JSON.stringify(credential),
});

storeTokens(result.accessToken, result.refreshToken);`} lang="typescript" />

  <h2>What if the passkey is lost</h2>
  <p>
    The user has a few escape hatches:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Recovery email.</strong> If the admin set
      <code>recovery_email</code> on <code>InviteUser</code>, a
      <code>RequestPasswordReset</code> sends a reset link to that
      address. The user picks a new password and uses it once.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Admin help.</strong> The user calls
      <code>RequestAdminHelp</code> with their email + a short reason. An
      admin resolves it via <code>ResolveHelpRequest</code>, which
      typically means issuing a one-time reset token through
      <code>ResetUserPassword</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A second passkey.</strong> Encourage users to register a
      second device immediately after the first; passkeys are unique to a
      device, but a user can have many.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
